Chapter: UsbKbdHub
  cites Works chapter GopUsb
  cites Works chapter GopUsbKbd
  cites Works chapter GopXhci
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 A keyboard behind a hub, driven for the first time.

 `usb-kbd-connect` drives `kbd-connect`, which walks the ROOT ports and
 nothing else. That is not the code the boot payload runs: `GopBoot`
 calls `usb-attach`, and `usb-attach` walks hubs. So the hub walk --
 `usb-open-hub`, `hub-walk`, `hub-attach-port`, and the transaction
 translator arithmetic in them -- had never been executed by anything,
 on any emulator, in any battery. It is the code that runs on the
 machine the campaign exists for, and it was the code nobody could see.

 codex-vm presents a high-speed hub on root port 4 with a FULL-SPEED
 boot keyboard on its one downstream port. That is the topology the
 hypothesis is about: a full-speed device below a high-speed hub reaches
 the bus only through that hub's transaction translator, and the
 controller learns which one from the TT Hub Slot ID and TT Port Number
 in the slot context. The model refuses to service a periodic endpoint
 whose slot names no translator, exactly as silicon does -- so a driver
 that leaves those fields zero enumerates perfectly and then hears
 nothing, which is the reported shape of the failure on the ASUS.

 The root keyboard is unplugged for this test (`-xhci-no-root-kbd`),
 because the walk takes the first keyboard it finds and root port 2
 comes first. Same guest binary, different bus.

 `usb-kbd-hub2` is the same question one tier deeper, where the hub the
 keyboard is plugged into is not the hub that translates for it.

 What each line proves. `route` non-zero says the keyboard was reached
 THROUGH the hub rather than off a root port -- without it every line
 below would be the root-attached case wearing this test's name.
 `speed=1` says it is full speed, so a translator is genuinely required.
 `code` is the completion, and it is the whole question: it can only be
 non-negative if the slot context named a transaction translator, because
 the controller declines to schedule the endpoint otherwise.

Section: Test

  found-line : UsbKbd -> Text
  found-line (k) =
    "kbd: ok=" & (if k.uk-ok then "1" else "0")
      & " dci=" & show (k.uk-dci)
      & " speed=" & show ((k.uk-dev).ud-speed)
      & " route=" & show ((k.uk-dev).ud-route)

  phase-of : UsbKbd -> Integer
  phase-of (k) = peek-32 (k.uk-state) 0

  opening : [Console, Device.Port, Device.Mmio] Nothing = act
    att <- usb-attach
    print-line-uni (found-line (att.ua-kbd))
    if (att.ua-kbd).uk-ok then act
      let p0 = kbd-pump (att.ua-kbd)
      in print-line-uni ("after-arm: phase=" & show (phase-of (att.ua-kbd)))
      let p1 = kbd-pump (att.ua-kbd)
      in print-line-uni ("after-check: phase=" & show (phase-of (att.ua-kbd)))
    end
    else print-line-uni "after-arm: SKIPPED (no keyboard)"
  end
